{"id":"crwv-earnings-q3-2026","title":"CRWV earnings — CoreWeave, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-11-10","html":"<p>Management sees far more demand than it can serve and rising prices through 2027; the thing that could spoil it is the cost of, and access to, the borrowed money that pays for the build.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-11-10. Next report: not found (one calendar site estimates Feb 25, 2027). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<p><strong>Fresh report: the Q3 10-Q was not yet filed when this was researched, so the risk-factor section is missing.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Management says qualified demand is about ten times available capacity through the end of 2027; backlog rose to $132.5B from $104.2B in one quarter.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised. 2026 revenue $13.0–13.3B (from $12.4–13.2B); adjusted operating income $1.275–1.375B (from $0.96–1.15B); capex $37–39B (low end up from $35B); year-end run-rate $20–21B (from $18.5–19.5B).</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> debt. Interest cost is guided to $1.05–1.15B for Q4 alone, on about $46.9B of debt at an average 7.9%.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans down still. Price is above its 20-day but well under the 50-day and 200-day; a close above $69.96 (my 38.2% line) would remove the lean.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $3.66B (+168%) vs $3.54B estimate, and above the top of its own $3.45–3.60B guide.</li><li>GAAP EPS -$1.27 vs -$1.55 estimate (Webull, GAAP against GAAP). Net loss $709M. Adjusted EPS against consensus: not found.</li><li>Interest expense $846M in the quarter. Adjusted operating margin went from about 1% to 5% to 10% over the first three quarters.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> Q4 interest expense $1.05–1.15B; year-end annualised revenue run-rate $20–21B; active power above 1.85GW (held).</li><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> $132.5B, up $28.3B in the quarter. More than 75% of committed 2027 capex is tied to investment-grade and enterprise customers.</li><li><strong>Pricing:</strong> rising. About 25% in July, another 10% later in the quarter and more in Q4; older-generation GPU prices up 30–50% in the quarter. Prepayments collected on 70% of 2026 deals.</li><li><strong>2027:</strong> qualitative only. Capex roughly level with 2026 if only the committed backlog is built; the adjusted loss shrinks; adjusted net income turns positive in the first half of 2028.</li><li><strong>Funding:</strong> &quot;we expect to raise less than $5 billion in incremental parent-level capital through 2027&quot; (Nitin Agrawal, CFO). The release also says about $9B of customer prepayments and deposits are expected over the next three months.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> active power above 1.7GW, contracted above 4.6GW.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Credit and capital market volatility.</strong> Interest above $1B a quarter from Q4; debt about $46.9B plus $18.7B of operating leases.</li><li><strong>Industry oversupply.</strong> Raised by an analyst on the call; management's answer was the ten-times demand figure.</li><li><strong>Permitting and construction limits.</strong> Capex ran at $14.5B against a $11.5–13.5B guide, so the build is moving, but sites and power are the constraint.</li><li><strong>Customer concentration.</strong> No named-customer figure in the Q3 material. An outside write-up (TIKR) puts the largest customer at 36% of June-quarter revenue, down from 71% a year earlier.</li><li><strong>Filing risk factors:</strong> not found (10-Q not yet filed).</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-the-cost-of-debt-both-ways\">The big worry: the cost of debt, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Customers paying up front. Prepayments on 70% of 2026 deals and about $9B more expected in three months cut how much has to be borrowed.</li><li>Higher prices. Each price rise lifts the cash earned on the same borrowed dollars; the adjusted margin has gone 1% → 5% → 10% this year.</li><li>Investment-grade customers behind the loans. The March $8.5B deal backed by such contracts priced at 2.25 points over the benchmark rate, far cheaper than its other borrowing.</li><li>The Fed stopping. Staff and Governor Bowman expect inflation to fall toward 2% next year; no official is arguing for cuts yet.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>More Fed hikes. Futures put about 90% odds on a December hike and traders expect two more by June (Fed Desk). Much of this debt floats with short rates.</li><li>Lenders asking for more. The August $2.6B loan priced at 5.5 points over the benchmark, widened from 4.25–4.5 during marketing (The Energy Mag). Its 2032 notes, sold at 9.625% in June, yielded over 16% by Nov 6 (TIKR).</li><li>A wider rethink of AI debt. Bowman flagged more than $800B of AI debt and circular financing as a risk; Chair Warsh says rates do bite on AI credit.</li><li>A big customer slowing or renegotiating. The backlog is only as good as the few names behind it.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Weakest balance sheet of the AI builders you track. S&amp;P rates the company B+ (investingLive); Microsoft funds its build from cash flow and issued no debt.</li><li>My arithmetic: one quarter of interest ($1.05–1.15B) is close to the whole year's adjusted operating income ($1.275–1.375B). The margin for error is thin.</li><li>In its favor: the loans are tied to signed contracts, prices are rising, and Nvidia's filing lists commitments to buy unsold capacity from AI cloud companies (the filing does not name which).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>Capex $14.5B in Q3 on the company's definition ($9.4B cash); 2026 guide $37–39B.</li><li>Paid for with contract-backed delayed-draw loans, about $4.2B of convertible notes, a first at-the-market share program, prepayments and vendor finance.</li><li>No buybacks, no dividend. Headcount: not found.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Michael Intrator, co-founder, CEO since 2017. He is also chairman, so the roles are not split. Lead independent director: Glenn Hutchins.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Nitin Agrawal, since March 2024.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> no. A board committee oversees succession.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> dual-class. Class B shares carry 10 votes each: 16.8% of shares, 66.9% of votes. Voting power at Apr 15, 2026: Intrator 38.7%, co-founders Venturo 20.3% and McBee 14.6% (sec-api.io summary of the proxy).</li><li><strong>Recent changes or investigations:</strong> securities class actions were filed in New Jersey federal court covering Mar 28–Dec 15, 2025, alleging overstated capacity and understated supplier delays, as reported by Fintool. The three co-founders have sold about $3.3B of stock since the IPO, $2.4B of it in 2026, as reported by Beinsure; the company called the sales pre-scheduled. Executive departures: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a lot. Three founders hold about three-quarters of the votes, one of them is both chairman and CEO, and they have been steady sellers.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"CRWV\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"CRWV · daily · my levels (not yours) chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">CRWV · daily · my levels (not yours)</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374